implementationPlan.description:
Test first (RED). Create packages/moe-daemon/src/tools/taskEventActor.test.ts with the Write tool. It is a tracked file, so never cp or heredoc it into the tree (global rail 3: a shell write leaves no TOOL witness and the path is HELD at landing).

The architect already validated this test against HEAD and against the fix. Read /mnt/d/moe-scratch/plan-e977/taskEventActor.test.ts.prototype and Write it as the new file. Add a short header comment that names task-e977c32b and its model, tools/reviewLoopActor.test.ts (mem:gotcha-review-loop-actor-regression). Keep its shape:
- Fixture, as in reviewLoopActor.test.ts: a temp .moe/ with epics, tasks, workers, proposals, teams, channels and messages; a TURBO project.json; epic-1. Five WORKING tasks, titled by their id:
  - task-c: worker-a, step-1 PENDING (add_comment and amend_plan_step);
  - task-rel: worker-r, step-1 COMPLETED (holder release, routes to REVIEW);
  - task-fix: unassigned, COMPLETED (the already-unassigned repair path, routes to REVIEW);
  - task-anon: worker-s, COMPLETED (caller-less release);
  - task-anon-fix: unassigned, COMPLETED (caller-less repair).
  Each assignee gets a CODING worker record. architect-z and governor-g need none: amend_plan_step's role gate falls back to the id prefix (util/workerRole.ts), and the repair path checks nothing about its caller. No release passes a handoffNote, so computeDiskStateSignature never shells out to git.
- Case 1, caller ids. Calls: add_comment {task-c, worker-a}; amend_plan_step {task-c, step-1, architect-z}; release_task {task-rel, worker-r}, which resolves with status REVIEW and releasedBy worker-r; release_task {task-fix, governor-g}, status REVIEW. Then flushActivityLog and these expect.soft checks, exact values:
  - the workerId of TASK_COMMENT_ADDED on task-c is ['worker-a']; of TASK_UPDATED on task-c, ['architect-z']; of WORKER_RELEASED on task-rel, ['worker-r']; on task-fix, ['governor-g'];
  - get_activity_log {workerId, limit: 100} for each seat, filtered to those three event types, is exactly ['TASK_COMMENT_ADDED task-c'], ['TASK_UPDATED task-c'], ['WORKER_RELEASED task-rel'] and ['WORKER_RELEASED task-fix'];
  - the #general 'Task moved to' lines are exactly ['task-rel: Task moved to REVIEW by worker-r', 'task-fix: Task moved to REVIEW by governor-g'].
- Case 2, no caller id. Calls: add_comment {task-c} with no workerId; release_task {task-anon}, whose RESULT keeps releasedBy 'unknown'; release_task {task-anon-fix}. Checks: each event's actor is [null] (map `e.workerId ?? null`); get_activity_log for 'unknown' and for 'agent' returns []; the notices are exactly ['task-anon: Task moved to REVIEW by unknown', 'task-anon-fix: Task moved to REVIEW by unknown'], today's fallback (DoD 2).
- The assertions are soft on purpose: each isolated mutant in step 4 must fail its own named check, not only the first.
- Reading #general right after the handler resolves is deterministic. sendMessage appends under a per-channel FIFO mutex (state/chatStore.ts), and release_task awaits its own banner post after updateTask has queued the notice.

Prove RED on HEAD 946358a8 (DoD 3) from the task worktree. The three tool files are byte-identical from 946358a8 to 6cca2104. Run exactly:
( set -o pipefail && R=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel) && S=$(mktemp -d /tmp/e977-red.XXXX) && git -C "$R" archive 946358a8 packages/moe-daemon scripts/moe-account-lease.mjs | tar -x -C "$S" && cp "$R/packages/moe-daemon/src/tools/taskEventActor.test.ts" "$S/packages/moe-daemon/src/tools/" && cd "$S/packages/moe-daemon" && npm ci --no-audit --no-fund >/dev/null 2>&1 && NO_COLOR=1 npx vitest run src/tools/taskEventActor.test.ts 2>&1 | grep -aE "AssertionError|Tests " )
Expect exit 1 and `Tests 1 failed | 1 passed (2)`. Case 1 fails on 9 soft checks: 4 actors, 4 seat queries and the notice list. Case 2 passes on HEAD, because it guards against an 'unknown' or 'agent' actor. The architect's own run is /mnt/d/moe-scratch/plan-e977/red-946358a8.txt. Paste the AssertionError lines into the step note.

Adversarial self-review of this row's diff, using the adversarial-self-review skill. Do it once, as the last step.
- Scope: `git diff` against the task base shows the four one-argument additions (9 changed lines in the three source files, matching /mnt/d/moe-scratch/plan-e977/fix.diff) and the new test. No other byte, no .moe/ path, and every tracked write was made with Edit or Write.
- Mutation check, in the step-3 snapshot and never the worktree: `bash /mnt/d/moe-scratch/plan-e977/mutants.sh <snapshot>/packages/moe-daemon`. It applies 7 mutants one at a time:
  - drop each of the 4 arguments;
  - release main path -> actors.actor;
  - comment -> comment.author;
  - repair path -> params.workerId || UNIDENTIFIED_RELEASER.
  It must print 7 `killed` lines, each naming its own assertion (M3 and M4 also name the notice check), then `restored fix: Tests 2 passed (2)`, and exit 0. Paste the output into the step note. The script refuses the shared checkout, edits only the snapshot it is given, and restores the fixed files after each mutant. The architect dry-ran it: 7 of 7 killed. A `NOT APPLIED` line means your fix is formatted differently from fix.diff: either match fix.diff, or apply that mutant by hand in the snapshot.
- Edge cases, confirmed by reading, not by new code:
  - a call with no caller id records no workerId (case 2);
  - a governor's forced strip records the governor, never the stripped holder;
  - an explicit workerId '' records workerId "", exactly as the review-loop tools have done since 3ba2bddd. That is not a false attribution, and normalizing it belongs in appendActivity, outside this row;
  - concurrency is unchanged: tool handlers serialize on the state mutex, and release_task already runs inside runExclusive;
  - error handling is unchanged: no new throw or IO path, and the best-effort activity write and chat posts still never fail a persisted comment, amendment or release.
- If any tracked file changes after step 3, rerun step 3 and cite the new output.
- Kill only PIDs you started (global rail 2). Delete only the exact snapshot paths your own runs printed.
